## What the family asks

The family follows the native definition in Sven Polak’s
[“Semidefinite programming bounds for constant weight codes”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05171).
The solver receives fixed-weight binary words, a coordinate permutation, and a
mandatory anchor word. It must select a stated number of words, including the
anchor, whose pairwise Hamming distances meet the threshold. The verifier reads
the selected words, recomputes every exact weight and distance, and accepts any
valid selection—not only the planted one.

Section 1 fixes the exact notions of word, weight, distance, and constant-weight
code. Section 1.2 supplies the load-bearing fact: simultaneous coordinate
permutations preserve weight and distance. The generator makes a full orbit of
the anchor first. Each word selects one point from every permutation cycle, so
the full orbit has disjoint supports and is a certified code. Hundreds of
independently sampled two-phase partial orbits provide near-miss decoys.

## Why Track B

This is not a Track A claim. A dictionary-based orbit index solves the generated
distribution in `O(NL + kL)` bit operations. Across eight shipping instances it
used 2,825,984 bit inspections (353,248 per instance) and 0.003534 seconds on
this host. Domain-standard Algorithm X also solved 8/8, using 4,297 search nodes,
3,798,441 row checks, and 0.355139 seconds. Once the Section 1.2 symmetry is
recognized, a no-tool solver can follow the anchor orbit and binary-search the
sorted table in 294 abstract exact operations; the difficulty is executing that
route accurately without text search or a sandbox.

The paper’s easy facts were checked before choosing this family. Proposition
1.1 maps a known code to feasible SDP data, and the paper says `A_k` is
polynomial-time computable for fixed `k` after symmetry reduction. Its largest
reported `B_4(22,8,10)` SDP nevertheless took about three weeks. The proposed
Golay route is not used: filtering the 2,048 words of the fixed shortened Golay
code is mechanically cheap and cannot yield an unlimited scalable Track A
family.

